  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened social dialogue mechanisms and labour administration systems for the good governance of work</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Effective social dialogue is critical for inclusive policy formulation, sound labour market governance, and stable labour relations in Zimbabwe. While the country has established a formal framework for tripartite engagement through the Tripartite Negotiating Forum (TNF), its effectiveness has been uneven, reflecting capacity constraints among the tripartite partners and broader institutional challenges. 
Tripartite partners often face limitations in technical expertise, coordination, evidence-based engagement, and resource availability, which affect their ability to participate meaningfully in social dialogue and to engage in effective collective bargaining. Strengthening these capacities&#x2014;including negotiation skills, research and data use, and organizational representativeness&#x2014;is essential to improving outcomes. In parallel, there is a need to reinforce labour administration and labour justice systems, particularly dispute resolution institutions, to ensure timely, credible, and accessible mechanisms for addressing industrial disputes and enforcing agreements.
Through the Decent Work Country Programme for Zimbabwe, the ILO supports efforts to enhance the functionality and impact of structured dialogue on key socio-economic issues and strengthening of regulatory frameworks and the capacities of labour administration and inspectorate systems. 
The ILO is also supporting efforts towards signing of a SOCIAL CONTRACT to deepen dialogue to advance decent work policies. Capacity building interventions on mutual gains negotiation skills (MGNS), collective bargaining and despite resolution are being implemented in the public and private sector. The Labour Inspectorates and the Future of Technology (LIFT) electronic case management platform is being implemented to assist authorities to map enterprises, detect risks, and track compliance.
These interventions aim to improve labour relations, enhance governance of the world of work, and strengthen protection at work for all.</narrative>
  </description>
